<code>assert this.indexOf(oldValue) != -1 || !\old(this.contains(oldValue));</code>

However, the comment suggests that if the value at the removed index occurred only once, it should no longer be present after removal. Therefore, the correct assertion should be:

<code>assert !\old(this.contains(oldValue)) || \old(this.indexOf(oldValue)) != \old(this.lastIndexOf(oldValue));</code>

But since the comment specifically states that the value should remain present if it occurred more than once, and should not be present if it occurred only once, the correct assertion should be:

<code>assert (\old(this.indexOf(oldValue)) == \old(this.lastIndexOf(oldValue)) && !this.contains(oldValue)) || (\old(this.indexOf(oldValue)) != \old(this.lastIndexOf(oldValue)) && this.contains(oldValue));</code>

This ensures that if the value occurred only once, it is no longer present, and if it occurred more than once, it remains present. However, given the typical interpretation of the comment, the simpler and more direct assertion would be:

<code>assert (\old(this.indexOf(oldValue)) == \old(this.lastIndexOf(oldValue))) => !this.contains(oldValue);</code>

This directly states that if the value occurred only once (i.e., its first and last occurrence indices are the same), it should no longer be present after removal.
